#{[17592186371425 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371425 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371425 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c9e77905-5ec8-56d5-8779-68fa5b781883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371426 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371426 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371426 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "97daab37-f8ae-5701-b85b-b2e30894a4f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371427 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371427 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371427 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b4e0b9ef-caf7-5749-a8a6-77538e234c6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371428 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371428 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371428 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5fd5a22-26d6-55d8-a7db-98cfc7d4ab6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371429 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371429 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371429 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371429 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371429 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5dfe9dfb-2de9-5049-bb36-b64e2c2a8946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371429 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371430 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "280b7aa8-2f12-5405-8f23-6f3ea0f0ea3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371431 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371431 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371431 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d74d4e6a-a2fb-53e8-8e78-2d14949a12fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371432 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371432 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371432 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6e1c4a83-8cdf-5b61-8446-22274e2c4d7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371433 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371433 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371433 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "253c1d32-31e5-545e-bf47-15607badcd6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371434 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371434 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371434 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "616c3f04-cce5-5e8c-a2fa-70633c80921f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371435 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371435 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371435 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "80f3af4a-ee17-5ae0-93d3-4ead53a6e48c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385970 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385970 #edb/ref 1061 648305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385970 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7dc02cbe-4a5d-5f2d-8ad3-baa5e8278916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385970 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186385971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385971 #edb/ref 1064 "In some singular value decomposition problems, we do not get all of the needed vectors from the usual formulas right away.\n\nThis happens, for example, when:\n\n- an eigenvalue is repeated, or\n\n- a singular value is zero, so the corresponding vector is not determined uniquely.\n\nIn these cases, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Solve the system that defines the eigenspace (or orthogonal complement).  \n\nFor example, we solve an equation of the form\n\n$$\n(A^T A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nor, when finding missing columns of $U,$ we solve orthogonality conditions such as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0, \\qquad \\mathbf{u}_2 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Write the general solution using free variables.  \n\nThis shows that there are infinitely many solutions. For example, the solution might look like\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = x\\,\\mathbf{b}_1 + y\\,\\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are free variables.\n\n- **Step 3.** Choose convenient vectors from the solution space.  \n\nWe usually choose simple values for the free variables to get easy vectors. These vectors should be linearly independent and, if possible, already orthogonal.\n\n- **Step 4.** Orthogonalize the chosen vectors if necessary.  \n\nIf the vectors are not mutually orthogonal, we apply the Gram–Schmidt process.\n\n- **Step 5.** Normalize the vectors.  \n\nAfter that, we divide each vector by its norm to obtain unit vectors.\n\nThese unit vectors form an orthonormal basis for the solution space, and they can be used as the needed columns of $V$ or $U.$\n\n**Note:** In these situations, there is usually *more than one correct answer*. Different choices of free variables can produce different orthonormal bases, so the resulting SVD is not necessarily unique. What matters is that the chosen vectors:\n\n- belong to the correct solution space,\n\n- are mutually orthogonal, and\n\n- have norm $1.$\n\nLet's see a concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385971 #edb/ref 1065 15621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385971 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5e93068a-566a-5c34-a459-3250d8a847d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385971 #edb/ref 1067 "Choosing Eigenvectors When Eigenvalues Repeat or Are Missing" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407928 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186408022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407928 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186408023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407928 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186408024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407928 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407928 #edb/ref 1045 "Singular Value Decomposition of Larger Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407928 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186408023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407928 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407928 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407928 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "069cd187-e43e-5701-8359-21c488d6fdf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407929 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{